  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\u2248f(a,b)+\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot \u27e8x-a,y-b\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(1,-1)+\u2207f(1,-1)\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y+1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-{y}^{2}\\, &  & \u21d2\\, & {f}_{x}(1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-2xy &  & \u21d2\\, & {f}_{y}(1,-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(1,-1) & =\u27e8-1,2\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(1,-1)+\u2207f(1,-1)\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y+1\u27e9 \\\\  & \u2248-1+\u27e8-1,2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-1,y+1\u27e9 \\\\  & \u2248-1-(x-1)+2(y+1) \\\\  & \u22482-x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
